{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Jacob blessed Pharaoh.</b> This is a greeting of peace, as is the way of all who appear before kings from time to time — the language of Rashi. But this does not appear correct, for it is not the way of royal etiquette that a person should greet the king with peace, as they said (Shabbat 89a): 'Is there a slave who gives peace to his master?' Rather, it was an actual blessing, for the way of elders and pious men who come before kings is to bless them with wealth and possessions and honor and the exaltation of their kingdom, as Scripture states (I Kings 1:31): 'Long live my master the king David forever.' And when he departed from before him, he turned and blessed him again, to take leave from before him. And our Rabbis said (Tanhuma Naso 26) that the Nile would rise to his foot."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:51.134298809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}